course: world
lesson: 98
title: Explorers and how the world was mapped
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Long ago nobody had a map of the whole world. Each people knew its own lands well, but far-off places were blank or filled with guesses. Explorers travelled into the unknown and brought back descriptions, so mapmakers could fill in the gaps bit by bit.
explain: Polynesian sailors crossed thousands of kilometres of the Pacific Ocean in canoes, finding islands by reading the stars, the waves and the flight of birds. Vikings reached North America about a thousand years ago, and Chinese fleets under Zheng He sailed to Africa in the early 1400s.
explain: In 1492 Christopher Columbus sailed west from Spain hoping to reach Asia, but he landed in the Caribbean, in lands Europeans did not know. Ferdinand Magellan's expedition set off in 1519, and in 1522 one of its ships came home — the first voyage all the way around the world. Magellan himself was killed on the way.
explain: To mark a place on a map, sailors needed its latitude (how far north or south) and its longitude (how far east or west). Latitude was found from the height of the Sun or the Pole Star in the sky. Longitude was much harder: the Earth turns a quarter-circle every six hours, so comparing local noon with a clock still set to home time shows how far east or west you are. Only in the 1700s did John Harrison build clocks good enough to keep time on a rocking ship.
explain: Today satellites photograph every part of the Earth, and GPS can tell your position to within a few metres — but the last great blank spaces, like the poles, were reached only about a hundred years ago.
fact: Polynesian navigators crossed the Pacific by reading the stars, the waves and the flight of birds.
fact: Vikings led by Leif Erikson reached North America about a thousand years ago.
fact: In the early 1400s Chinese fleets led by Zheng He sailed as far as East Africa.
fact: In 1492 Columbus sailed west hoping to reach Asia but reached islands in the Caribbean.
fact: Columbus thought he had reached the Indies in Asia, so he called the people he met «Indians».
fact: Magellan's expedition made the first voyage around the world, from 1519 to 1522; Magellan died on the way.
fact: Captain James Cook mapped the coasts of New Zealand and eastern Australia around 1770.
fact: Lewis and Clark crossed the western United States to the Pacific Ocean between 1804 and 1806.
fact: Roald Amundsen's team was the first to reach the South Pole, in 1911.
fact: Edmund Hillary and Tenzing Norgay were the first to stand on top of Mount Everest, in 1953.
fact: Latitude tells how far north or south a place is; longitude tells how far east or west.
fact: The farther north a ship sails, the higher the Pole Star stands in the sky.
fact: The Earth turns 15 degrees of longitude every hour, so a difference in time shows a difference in longitude; if the home clock is later than your local noon, you are west of home.
fact: John Harrison built clocks in the 1700s accurate enough to find longitude at sea.
exercise: Why did old maps have blank spaces? | Nobody who made the map had been to those places | Mapmakers ran out of ink and paper | Those places were covered by the sea | Kings forbade drawing certain lands
exercise: How did Polynesian sailors find islands across the Pacific? | By reading the stars, waves and birds | By using a magnetic compass and charts | By following radio signals from land | By sailing along the coast all the way
exercise: Who reached North America about a thousand years ago? | Vikings led by Leif Erikson | Spanish sailors led by Columbus | English sailors led by James Cook | Chinese sailors led by Zheng He
exercise: Where was Columbus trying to go when he sailed west in 1492? | Asia | Australia | The South Pole | Africa
exercise: What did Magellan's expedition achieve? | The first voyage around the world | The first long crossing of Australia | The first trip to the South Pole | The first map of the Moon
exercise: Did Magellan himself finish the voyage around the world? | No, he was killed on the way | Yes, he sailed every kilometre | Yes, but he came home by land | No, he turned back after a week
exercise: Which coasts did Captain James Cook map around 1770? | New Zealand and eastern Australia | Greenland and the far north of Canada | West Africa and South America | China and Japan
exercise: Who first reached the South Pole? | Roald Amundsen's team in 1911 | Robert Falcon Scott's team in 1912 | Lewis and Clark on their trek in 1806 | Leif Erikson's Vikings about 1000
exercise: What does latitude tell you? | How far north or south a place is | How far east or west a place is | How high above the sea a place is | How many people live in a place
exercise: How did sailors find their latitude? | From the height of the Sun or Pole Star | From the colour and saltiness of the sea water | From the number of days they had been sailing | From the direction and strength of the wind
exercise: Why was longitude so hard to find at sea? | Ships needed a clock that kept exact time | Ships could not see the stars from out at sea | The Sun never rises high at sea | Compasses do not work on water
exercise: How many degrees of longitude does the Earth turn in one hour? | 15 | 1 | 24 | 360
exercise: What did John Harrison invent to help sailors? | Clocks accurate enough to keep time at sea | The very first magnetic compass for big ships | A strong telescope for seeing far-off islands | Maps printed on special waterproof paper for storms
exercise: How are maps made today? | From satellite pictures and GPS | Only from what sailors describe | By guessing from old drawings | By walking every road with a ruler
test: A sailor measures the Pole Star higher and higher in the sky each night. Which way is the ship sailing? | North | South | East | West
test: Columbus called the people he met «Indians». What does this name show about his belief? | He thought he had reached Asia | He thought he had reached Africa | He knew he was in a new continent | He thought he had sailed home
test: Which explorer's journey happened first? | Leif Erikson's voyage to North America | Columbus's first voyage to the Caribbean | Cook's mapping of New Zealand | Amundsen's journey to the South Pole
test: A ship returned home in 1522 after sailing all the way round the Earth. What did this prove to everyone? | That the oceans join and the Earth can be sailed round | That the Earth is flat with an edge | That Asia and America are the same land | That the Sun goes around the Earth
test: At local noon a navigator's clock, set to home time, shows 3 o'clock in the afternoon. Where is the ship? | 45 degrees west of home | 45 degrees east of home | 3 degrees west of home | Exactly at home
test: Why did the poles and Everest stay unreached long after the coasts were mapped? | The cold, ice and height made them deadly hard to reach | Nobody knew that the poles existed at all | Kings did not allow anyone to go there | They were hidden under the ocean
test: A clock on a ship loses one hour during a long voyage. What happens to the longitude the sailors work out? | It is wrong by 15 degrees | It is still exactly right | It is wrong by 1 degree | It is wrong by 360 degrees
test: Which is true about Polynesian navigators? | They found tiny islands without compasses | They sailed only along coasts, within sight of land | They used maps made by Captain Cook | They crossed the ocean by steamship
test: Why can we now map places no explorer ever walked? | Satellites photograph the whole Earth from space | Every place has now been walked by explorers | Old maps were already complete | Ships can sail over mountains today
test: who got to the south pole first | Roald Amundsen's team, in 1911 | Captain James Cook, around 1770 | Edmund Hillary, in 1953 | Lewis and Clark, in 1806
test: how'd sailors work out longitude at sea back then | With an accurate clock comparing local time with home time | By counting the stars at midnight | By measuring how deep the sea was | By looking at the colour of the water
test: Leo says Columbus was the first European to reach America. true? | No, Vikings led by Leif Erikson reached it about 500 years earlier | Yes, no European had been there before | Yes, he landed in North America in 1492 | No, Magellan got there first
test: my sister reckons Magellan sailed all the way round the world himself. right? | No, his expedition did, but Magellan died on the way | Yes, he sailed round and came home a hero | Yes, he did it twice | No, the voyage never got around the world
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the voyage of Columbus in 1492? | He sailed west hoping to reach Asia but reached islands in the Caribbean | He sailed east and reached the coast of China | He sailed around the world and returned to Spain | He sailed south and reached the South Pole
exam: Identify the team which first reached the South Pole, together with the year thereof. | Roald Amundsen's team, in 1911 | Captain Robert Scott's team, in 1912 | Magellan's team, in 1522 | Hillary and Norgay, in 1953
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning latitude and longitude. | Latitude tells how far north or south; longitude how far east or west | Latitude tells how far east or west; longitude how far north or south | Both tell only how high a place is | Both tell only how far a place is from the sea
exam: It is given that the Earth turns 15 degrees of longitude every hour. Determine the difference in longitude between two places whose local times differ by two hours. | Thirty degrees | Fifteen degrees | Sixty degrees | Seven and a half degrees
